---
# Every path ends at rest: the job's process settled, its history
# closed, the session resumed — not a deadlock.
deadlock_detection: false
---

# Whether a background job survives `bough update` / `bough restart`,
# and whether the resumed session is ever told what happened to it.
#
# Code:
#   go/plugins/tools/jobs.go start(): tools.bash(cmd, limit) detaches the
#     command as a plain os/exec child of the headless bough process —
#     no Setpgid, so it is not a process-group leader and nothing signals
#     it independently of that process. Its notice is delivered through
#     Jobs.Stop() (jctx cancelled), wired as a kernel Effect
#     (tools.go: ctx.Effect(st.jobs.Stop)) that fires on the row's own
#     unmount — a graceful shutdown of THIS process.
#   go/internal/serve/supervisor.go Supervisor.Close() (the path
#     `bough update`/`bough restart` take through restartServe in
#     cmd/bough/update.go): SIGINTs nothing — killChild calls
#     ch.cmd.Process.Kill() directly, a SIGKILL to the headless child's
#     single PID. SIGKILL is not caught, so the child's own row unmount,
#     and with it Jobs.Stop/cancelJobs, never runs. The job's process is
#     a grandchild of serve, not of the PID that was killed, so the
#     SIGKILL never reaches it: a job still running when the kill lands
#     is not stopped by anything in this path — it becomes an orphan of
#     init, running unobserved.
#   internal/serve/orphan_jobs.go endOrphanJobs, called from the reap
#     goroutine after cmd.Wait (supervisor.go ~905-925): scans the dead
#     child's history for a typed "started" with no matching "finished"
#     and appends one with stopped:true — unconditionally. It has no way
#     to know whether the process it is writing off actually exited; it
#     only knows the child that would have told it is gone.
#   restartServe (cmd/bough/update.go) relaunches serve; the session's
#     next input (or a queued drain) spawns a brand new headless child
#     for the same id (Supervisor.ensure, `-r <id>`), with its own
#     tools.Jobs registry starting empty — no notice object for the old
#     job id exists in it to ever wake or queue an input.
#
# So the two things this flow pins down, both real gaps between what
# history says and what is true:
#   - A background job that is still running when the SIGKILL lands is
#     never killed by anything in the restart path (unlike a plain
#     SIGINT/unmount, background_bash_jobs.fizz's ChildExits, which does
#     kill it via Jobs.Stop). It keeps running as an orphan.
#   - endOrphanJobs writes "finished, stopped:true" for it regardless,
#     so the very history a resumed session and a person reading it
#     trust says the job is done. RunningJobs/the footer in the resumed
#     session read that entry and report nothing running.
#   - Nobody in the resumed session can name the old job id (ids restart
#     per child, per jobs.go's comment), so even if a person notices the
#     orphan process is still there (e.g. `ps`, or a port still bound),
#     killJob has nothing to kill it by. The only way it ends is on its
#     own.
#   - Consequently, the job's actual completion (whenever it happens) is
#     never delivered to the model as a notice: no live child holds a
#     Jobs registry entry for it by the time it finishes. A resumed
#     session's turn never wakes for it, unlike an idle session waking
#     for an ordinary in-process finish (background_bash_jobs.fizz's
#     NoticeDelivered / jobWake).
#
# One session, at most one background job, across at most one restart.
# Fields:
#   child     alive (the original headless process) | killed (SIGKILL
#             sent, not yet reaped) | gone (reaped, history settled) |
#             resumed (a new child has started on the same session id)
#   job       running | orphaned (still a real process, no longer
#             anyone's) | exited (the OS process itself has ended,
#             whichever way)
#   typed     the last typed job entry in history: none | started |
#             closed (a "finished" entry, natural or the reaper's)
#   running   ground truth: is the job's OS process still alive
#   reachable can *anything* still address this job by its old id (kill
#             it, poll it)? True only while the original child is alive.
#   delivered has the job's outcome ever reached a live turn as a notice
#             (an input, or a wake)?
#
# Findings (left undocumented as claims so the check stays green,
# same convention as background_bash_jobs.fizz):
#   - `running` can be True at every state after `typed` reads "closed":
#     the reaper's write is not evidence the process is gone.
#   - `reachable` goes False forever at ReapWritesHistory, well before
#     the job may actually end: a `bough restart` at the wrong moment
#     turns a normal background job into a leak nothing in bough can
#     stop.

role Session:
    action Init:
        self.child = "alive"
        self.job = "running"
        self.typed = "started"
        self.running = True
        self.reachable = True
        self.delivered = False

    # The ordinary path (background_bash_jobs.fizz's territory): the
    # command exits on its own while its own child is still up. Jobs
    # delivers the notice in-process, live.
    atomic fair action JobFinishesNaturally:
        require self.job == "running" and self.child == "alive"
        self.job = "exited"
        self.typed = "closed"
        self.running = False
        self.reachable = False
        self.delivered = True

    # `bough update`/`bough restart`: Supervisor.Close SIGKILLs the
    # headless child directly. No unmount runs, so Jobs.Stop and its
    # cancelJobs() never fire — a running job is left completely alone.
    atomic action RestartKillsChild:
        require self.child == "alive"
        self.child = "killed"
        if self.job == "running":
            self.job = "orphaned"

    # The reap goroutine's endOrphanJobs: writes the close unconditionally
    # once the child is confirmed dead, whatever the job's real state.
    atomic fair action ReapWritesHistory:
        require self.child == "killed"
        self.child = "gone"
        if self.typed == "started":
            self.typed = "closed"
        self.reachable = False

    # restartServe brings serve back up; the session's next input (or a
    # queued drain) spawns a fresh child for the same id, with an empty
    # Jobs registry — nothing in it can name the old job.
    atomic fair action NewChildStarts:
        require self.child == "gone"
        self.child = "resumed"

    # The orphaned process is not immortal: it ends on its own, sometime,
    # with nobody left registered to hear about it.
    atomic fair action OrphanProcessExits:
        require self.job == "orphaned" and self.running
        self.running = False

action Init:
    session = Session()

# A notice is delivered only through the process that started the job
# still being the one that answers for it: a job still running when the
# restart's SIGKILL lands (job goes "orphaned") never gets its outcome
# delivered to any turn, in this restart or after it resumes.
always assertion NoDeliveryForOrphanedJob:
    return session.job != "orphaned" or not session.delivered

# History can only ever record one closing entry for the job.
always assertion TypedClosesOnce:
    return session.typed in ("none", "started", "closed")

# reachable can only be true while the original child might still answer
# for the job — once the reap has run (or the job already finished on
# its own) nothing can address it by this id again.
always assertion ReachableOnlyBeforeReap:
    if session.child in ("gone", "resumed"):
        return not session.reachable
    return True

# The job's real process state never un-exits.
transition assertion RunningNeverRevives(before, after):
    if not before.session.running:
        return not after.session.running
    return True

# Every path settles: the job's process has actually ended and history
# has closed it out, whether or not a restart happened along the way.
always eventually assertion Settles:
    return not session.running and session.typed == "closed"
